WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.689
Bad news, Marvel nerds: many of your favorite
characters were probably stolen from your

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:08.980
arch-nemesis.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:09.980
It's true.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.589
DC Comics has been churning out awesome characters
since 1934.

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:17.840
By the time Marvel Comics launched in 1939
under their first name, Timely Comics, they

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.380
were already five years behind.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.519
Sure, Marvel is great, but they have quite
a few characters of...questionable origin.

00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:28.320
Don't worry—DC has a few Marvel rip-offs
of its own, which you can learn about in our

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.670
companion video, "Characters DC Stole From
Marvel And Gave A New Name."

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:35.750
But for now, here are some of Marvel's greatest
characters, pitted against their earlier DC

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.260
counterparts.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.780
The X-Men vs. The Doom Patrol

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.620
It's a comic about a team of misfits who've
been shunned by society, coming together and

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.559
learning to use their powers for good.

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:49.059
They fight for acceptance under the leadership
of a brainiac in a wheelchair.

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:53.920
They're called...The Doom Patrol, and they
were first published in June of 1963—three

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.879
months before the X-Men showed up.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:00:56.879
Coincidence?

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:01.000
The similarities between the two teams would
grow in March of 1964, when the X-Men took

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.969
on the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants.

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:06.140
Meanwhile, the Doom Patrol fought The Brotherhood
of Evil.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.850
The similarities between the two comics were
so strong that Doom Patrol creator Arnold

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.409
Drake accused Stan Lee of somehow stealing
his ideas, though it's never been proven.

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:15.830
Maybe great minds think alike.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.180
Or just have great mind-reading super powers.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.610
Black Cat vs. Catwoman

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.700
Originally known as simply "The Cat," Catwoman
appeared in the first issue of Batman, published

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.049
in 1940.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:28.970
She was originally a cat-themed burglar with
no fancy powers.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:34.280
And today, Catwoman Selina Kyle is still pretty
much the same: an agile anti-hero with a romantic

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.259
interest in Batman.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:40.350
Writer Marv Wolfman basically ripped off the
whole "literal cat burglar" Catwoman thing

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.899
when he created Felicia Hardy, the Black Cat,
in 1979.

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:47.750
From the cat-themed, morally ambiguous thief
aspects, all the way down to her weird love

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:51.540
of Spider-Man, Black Cat lacks any kind of
real originality.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.530
Over time, Black Cat got some vague "bad luck"
powers and a super-suit, but Felicia "Black

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.829
Cat" Hardy still has her origins as a cheap
Catwoman ripoff.

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:00.869
"Bye Felicia"

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:02.770
Deadpool vs. Deathstroke

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:07.210
Rob Liefeld has a bit of a reputation for
not being able to come up with truly original

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.210
ideas.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.450
When the Teen Titans fan showed his first
drawings of Deadpool to writer Fabian Nicieza

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:16.680
in 1991, it was obvious that Deadpool wasn't
much more than an unoriginal "homage" to DC

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.150
Comics' Deathstroke.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.550
And Nicieza ran with it.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:24.170
The terrible joke expanded to the fact that
Deadpool was eventually named Wade Wilson...after

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.100
Deathstroke's real name, Slade Wilson.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.620
Ultimately, Deadpool was such a silly character
that he took on a life of his own, becoming

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.800
a fan favorite, and embraced the most absurd
comic adventures ever written.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.340
With his smash movie from 2016, he's become
a cultural phenomenon.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:41.430
Meanwhile, Deathstroke is still just a boring
assassin obsessed with killing super-teens.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.230
But at least he was there first.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.810
The Vision vs. Red Tornado

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.930
You may know Vision from Avengers: Age of
Ultron.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.900
But he first appeared in the comics all the
way back in 1968.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.960
The original Vision is an evil creation of
Ultron designed to destroy the Avengers.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.720
And just like in Age of Ultron, Vision ultimately
turns against his creator and becomes a hero

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.410
as he searches for his humanity.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.220
However, this wasn’t the first time this
story was told.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.550
A few years earlier in 1963, a very similar
event occurred when a supervillain designed

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.690
an android called Red Tornado.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.280
The bot was created to infiltrate and defeat
DC’s Justice Society of America, but ultimately

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.860
ended up becoming a hero as well.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.460
Yes, team-destroying androids are common,
and the two have different powers.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.630
But their costumes have just a few too many
similarities to be purely coincidental.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:28.530
Those big belts and enormous collars are way
too gauche to just be accidents…

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.600
Bullseye vs. Deadshot

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.530
Deadshot first appears in 1950 as a Batman
villain, where he’s first seen wearing a

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.960
top hat while attempting to replace Batman
as the hero of Gotham.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.980
Deadshot basically has Batman’s fighting
skills, but brings expert marksmanship the

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:42.980
table.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.420
Later, in the world of Marvel, another street-level
hero, Daredevil, would face a similar foe

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.420
with Bullseye.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.360
But that wouldn’t happen until 1976.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.040
Both Deadshot and Bullseye are master marksmen
who never miss their targets.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.420
"I never miss"

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.980
They’re experts at hand-to-hand combat,
have metal body enhancements, and come from

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.430
abusive families.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.900
The two are basically a couple of sad sacks
with really good aim, and that aim is good

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.550
enough to keep both of them working in comics
and on screen… but Deadshot was first...by

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.860
a longshot.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.040
Boomerang vs. Captain Boomerang

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.660
Why Marvel comics would want to mimic DC Comics'
boomerang-tossing bad guy is anyone's guess,

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.209
because both of them... kinda suck.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.160
Obviously, both characters were born in Australia,
but while Captain Boomerang primarily fights

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.830
the Flash, regular Boomerang spends a lot
of time fighting Marvel's fast-talking Spider-Man.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:33.310
The Captain arrived in 1960, while his unranked
Marvel doppelganger appeared in 1966.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:37.260
Both use ridiculous augmented boomerangs that
include everything from bombs to razors to

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.190
probably bumblebees or something.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:43.570
And Marvel's Boomerang even wears a boomerang
on his face, like angry eyebrows, just so

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:44.830
everyone knows what he does.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.210
Hey at least Captain Boomerang keeps it limited
to a really terrible boomerang-print shirt.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.210
Have we said the word "boomerang" enough yet?

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.210
Boomerang.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.690
Super Adaptoid vs. Amazo

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.070
The idea of an android that could copy the
powers of superheroes was created by Gardner

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.730
Fox and Murphy Anderson in 1960.

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.960
They named him Amazo, and he became a pretty
big threat to the Justice League of America.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.480
Six years later, Stan Lee came up with a strikingly
similar idea, sending a robot foe with identical

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.780
power-copying abilities up against the Avengers.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.500
"But from where did it come?"

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.040
"Ha ha ha ha!

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.960
Cannot you guess, Captain America?"

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.660
Granted, Lee's Super Adaptoid looks way cooler
than Amazo's weird elf-ears and striped-pants

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.660
thing, but that's all thanks to Jack Kirby's
awesome art.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.760
Unfortunately, Super-Adaptoid was just six
years too late to be the original.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.910
Thanos vs. Darkseid

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:35.300
Darkseid is Jack Kirby's giant-jawed alien
overlord of Apokolips, hell-bent on seeking

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.040
out the anti-life equation and eradicating
the universe of all free will.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:43.540
Thanos, on the other hand, is the giant-jawed
overlord of Titan, fixated on making Death

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.090
his girlfriend and subjugating the galaxy.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:50.380
Both are super-strong, telekinetic teleporters
who seem to be ageless, and can travel through

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.380
time.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.870
And both are pretty much the most powerful
bad guys in their respective universes.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:57.930
You can't really blame Jim Starlin for trying
to come up with his own version of Darkseid

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:02.139
for Marvel Comics in 1973, because Darkseid
is one heck of an awesome villain.

00:06:02.139 --> 00:06:06.150
It doesn't get much cooler than soul-seeking
eye-lasers, but those eye-lasers showed up

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:10.250
in 1970, three years prior to Marvel’s Thanos
and his Infinity Gauntlet.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.639
Even the Time Gem can’t fix that.

00:06:11.639 --> 00:06:12.639
Thanks for watching!

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:15.860
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.870
And leave us a comment to let us know which
characters you think are the biggest ripoffs...

